# 5. Terraform Basic Workflow

Three main steps:

## 1. Initialize

Downloads providers.

```bash id="4x7n1m"
terraform init
```

---

## 2. Plan

Shows changes.

```bash id="7m2q8p"
terraform plan
```

---

## 3. Apply

Creates infrastructure.

```bash id="9n4x2k"
terraform apply
```

---

# 6. Terraform Project Structure

Production structure:

```text id="v6m3q8"

eks-project/

├── provider.tf

├── variables.tf

├── main.tf

├── outputs.tf

├── versions.tf

└── modules/

    ├── vpc/

    ├── eks/

    └── iam/
```

---

# 7. Terraform Provider

Provider tells Terraform where to create resources.

Example:

```hcl id="5x7k9m"
provider "aws" {

 region = "ap-south-1"

}
```

---

# 8. Terraform Resource

A resource represents AWS infrastructure.

Example:

```hcl id="q2m8x4"
resource "aws_s3_bucket" "backup" {

 bucket = "eks-backup"

}
```

---

# 9. Terraform Variables

Instead of hardcoding values:

Bad:

```hcl id="1q9m5z"
instance_type = "t3.medium"
```

Better:

```hcl id="6m4p8x"
variable "instance_type" {

 default = "t3.medium"

}
```

---

# 10. Terraform State

Terraform stores infrastructure information in:

```text id="h5q8m2"
terraform.tfstate
```

It tracks:

* Created resources
* Resource IDs
* Current configuration

---

# 11. Remote State

Production teams store state remotely.

Common backend:

* Amazon S3
* DynamoDB locking

Architecture:

```text id="c7m9q3"

Developer

    |

Terraform

    |

S3 Backend

    |

State File

```

Benefits:

* Team collaboration
* Locking
* Backup

